    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-380",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 198,
      "pageEnd": 198,
      "target": "../sections/sec-380-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The Package URL core specification defines a versioned and formalized format, syntax, and rules used to represent and validate\npackage URLs.\nA package URL or purl is an attempt to standardize existing approaches to reliably identify the location of software packages.\nA purl is a URL string used to identify the location of a software package in a mostly universal and uniform way across programming\nlanguages, package managers, packaging conventions, tools, APIs and databases.\nSuch a package URL is useful to reliably reference the same software package using a simple and expressive syntax and con",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-381",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Syntax definition",
      "pageStart": 198,
      "pageEnd": 199,
      "target": "../sections/sec-381-syntax-definition.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "purl stands for package URL.\nA purl is a URL composed of seven components:\nscheme:type/namespace/name@version?qualifiers#subpath\nComponents are separated by a specific character for unambiguous parsing.\nThe definition for each components is:\n• scheme: this is the URL scheme with the constant value of “pkg”. One of the primary reason for this single scheme is to\nfacilitate the future official registration of the “pkg” scheme for package URLs. Required.\n• type: the package type or package protocol such as maven, npm, nuget, gem, pypi, etc. Required.\n• namespace: some name prefix such as a Maven",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-382",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Character encoding",
      "pageStart": 199,
      "pageEnd": 199,
      "target": "../sections/sec-382-character-encoding.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "For clarity and simplicity a purl is always an ASCII string. To ensure that there is no ambiguity when parsing a purl, separator\ncharacters and non-ASCII characters must be encoded in UTF-8, and then percent-encoded as defined in RFC 3986 https://datatr\nacker.ietf.org/doc/rfc3986.\nUse these rules for percent-encoding and decoding purl components:\n• the type must NOT be encoded and must NOT contain separators\n• the #, ?, @ and : characters must NOT be encoded when used as separators. They may need to be encoded elsewhere\n• the : scheme and type separator does not need to and must NOT be encoded",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-383",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rules for each component",
      "pageStart": 199,
      "pageEnd": 199,
      "target": "../sections/sec-383-rules-for-each-component.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A purl string is an ASCII URL string composed of seven components.\nSome components are allowed to use other characters beyond ASCII: these components must then be UTF-8-encoded strings and\npercent-encoded as defined in the “Character encoding” section.\nThe rules for each component are:\nE.4.1",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-384",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rules for scheme",
      "pageStart": 199,
      "pageEnd": 199,
      "target": "../sections/sec-384-rules-for-scheme.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "• The scheme is a constant with the value “pkg”\n• Since a purl never contains a URL Authority, its scheme must not be suffixed with double slash as in pkg:// and should\nuse instead pkg:.\n• purl parsers must accept URLs such as ‘pkg://’ and must ignore the ‘//’.\n• purl builders must not create invalid URLs with such double slash ‘//’.\n• The scheme is followed by a ‘:’ separator.\n• For example, the two purls pkg:gem/ruby-advisory-db-check@0.12.4 and pkg://gem/ruby-advisory-db-chec\nare strictly equivalent. The first is in canonical form while the second is an acceptable purl but is an invalid URI",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-385",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rules for type",
      "pageStart": 199,
      "pageEnd": 199,
      "target": "../sections/sec-385-rules-for-type.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "• The package type is composed only of ASCII letters and numbers, ., + and - (period, plus, and dash).\n• The type cannot start with a number.\n• The type cannot contain spaces.\n• The type must not be percent-encoded.\n• The type is case insensitive, with the canonical form being lowercase.\nE.4.3",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-386",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rules for namespace",
      "pageStart": 199,
      "pageEnd": 200,
      "target": "../sections/sec-386-rules-for-namespace.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "• The optional namespace contains zero or more segments, separated by slash /.\n• Leading and trailing slashes / are not significant and should be stripped in the canonical form. They are not part of the\nnamespace.\n• Each namespace segment must be a percent-encoded string.\n• When percent-decoded, a segment must not contain a slash / and must not be empty.\n• A URL host or Authority must NOT be used as a namespace. Use instead a repository_url qualifier. Note however\nthat for some types, the namespace may look like a host.\nSystem Package Data Exchange (SPDX©) v3.0\n187\nE.4.4\nRules for name\n• The n",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-387",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rules for version",
      "pageStart": 200,
      "pageEnd": 200,
      "target": "../sections/sec-387-rules-for-version.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "• The version is prefixed by a at-sign @ separator when not empty.\n• This at-sign @ is not part of the version.\n• A version must be a percent-encoded string.\n• A version is a plain and opaque string. Some package types use versioning conventions such as SemVer for NPMs or NEVRA\nconventions for RPMS. A type may define a procedure to compare and sort versions, but there is no reliable and uniform\nway to do such comparison consistently.\nE.4.6",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-388",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rules for qualifiers",
      "pageStart": 200,
      "pageEnd": 200,
      "target": "../sections/sec-388-rules-for-qualifiers.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "• The qualifiers string is prefixed by a ? separator when not empty.\n• This ? is not part of the qualifiers.\n• This is a string composed of zero or more key=value pairs each separated by an ampersand &. A key and value are separated\nby an equal = character.\n• These & are not part of the key=value pairs.\n• Each key must be unique within the keys of the qualifiers string.\n• A value cannot be an empty string; a key=value pair with an empty value is the same as no key/value at all for this key.\n• Each key must be composed only of ASCII letters and numbers, ., - and \\_ (period, dash and underscore)",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-389",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rules for subpath",
      "pageStart": 200,
      "pageEnd": 200,
      "target": "../sections/sec-389-rules-for-subpath.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "• The subpath string is prefixed by a # separator when not empty.\n• This # is not part of the subpath.\n• The subpath contains zero or more segments, separated by slash /.\n• Leading and trailing slashes / are not significant and should be stripped in the canonical form.\n• Each subpath segment must be a percent-encoded string.\n• When percent-decoded, a segment must not contain a /, must not be any of .. or ., and must not be empty.\n• The subpath must be interpreted as relative to the root of the package.\nE.5",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-390",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Known types",
      "pageStart": 200,
      "pageEnd": 200,
      "target": "../sections/sec-390-known-types.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "There are several known purl package type definitions. The current list of known types is: alpm, apk, bitbucket, bitnami,\ncargo, cocoapods, composer, conan, conda, cpan, cran, deb, docker, gem, generic, github, golang,\nhackage, hex, huggingface, luarocks, maven, mlflow, npm, nuget, oci, pub, pypi, qpkg, rpm, swid, and\nswift.\nThe list, with definitions for each type, is maintained in the file named PURL-TYPES.rst in the online repository\nhttps://github.com/package-url/purl-spec.\nE.6",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-391",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Known qualifiers key/value pairs",
      "pageStart": 200,
      "pageEnd": 201,
      "target": "../sections/sec-391-known-qualifiers-keyvalue-pairs.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Qualifiers should be limited to the bare minimum for proper package identification, to ensure that a purl stays compact and readable\nin most cases. Separate external attributes stored outside of a purl are the preferred mechanism to convey extra long and optional\ninformation. API, database or web form.\nThe following keys are valid for use in all package types:\n• repository_url is an extra URL for an alternative, non-default package repository or registry. The default repository\nor registry of each type is documented in the “Known types” section.\n188\nSystem Package Data Exchange (SPDX©) v3.0\n•",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-392",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How to produce and consume purl data",
      "pageStart": 201,
      "pageEnd": 201,
      "target": "../sections/sec-392-how-to-produce-and-consume-purl-data.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The following provides rules to be followed when building or deconstructing purl instances.\nE.7.1",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-393",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How to build purl string from its components",
      "pageStart": 201,
      "pageEnd": 202,
      "target": "../sections/sec-393-how-to-build-purl-string-from-its-components.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Building a purl ASCII string works from left to right, from type to subpath.\nTo build a purl string from its components:\n1. Start a purl string with the “pkg:” scheme as a lowercase ASCII string\n2. Append the type string to the purl as a lowercase ASCII string\n3. Append / to the purl\n4. If the namespace is not empty:\n1. Strip the namespace from leading and trailing /\n2. Split on / as segments\n3. Apply type-specific normalization to each segment, if needed\n4. Encode each segment in UTF-8-encoding\n5. Percent-encode each segment\n6. Join the segments with /\n7. Append this to the purl\n8. Append / t",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-394",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How to parse a purl string to its components",
      "pageStart": 202,
      "pageEnd": 203,
      "target": "../sections/sec-394-how-to-parse-a-purl-string-to-its-components.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Parsing a purl ASCII string into its components works by splitting the string on different characters.\nTo parse a purl string in its components:\n1. Split the purl string once from right on #, if present; the left side is the remainder.\n2. If the right side is not empty, it contains subpath information:\n1. Strip it from leading and trailing /.\n2. Split this on / in a list of segments.\n3. Discard empty, ., and .. segments.\n4. Percent-decode each segment.\n5. UTF-8-decode each of these.\n6. Join segments with /.\n7. This is the subpath.\n3. Split the remainder once from right on ?, if present; the le",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/omg-2024-spdx-3-0/blocks/prose-sec-395",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Examples",
      "pageStart": 203,
      "pageEnd": 203,
      "target": "../sections/sec-395-examples.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The following list includes some valid purl examples:\n• pkg:bitbucket/birkenfeld/pygments-main@244fd47e07d1014f0aed9c\n• pkg:deb/debian/curl@7.50.3-1?arch=i386&distro=jessie\n• pkg:gem/ruby-advisory-db-check@0.12.4\n• pkg:github/package-url/purl-spec@244fd47e07d1004f0aed9c\n• pkg:golang/google.golang.org/genproto#googleapis/api/annotations\n• pkg:maven/org.apache.xmlgraphics/batik-anim@1.9.1?packaging=sources\n• pkg:npm/foobar@12.3.1\n• pkg:nuget/EnterpriseLibrary.Common@6.0.1304\n• pkg:pypi/django@1.11.1\n• pkg:rpm/fedora/curl@7.50.3-1.fc25?arch=i386&distro=fedora-25\nE.9",
